2}-3x+7\\ge 5x-8$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-27011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81cf9097a0d21d1b4f4cc54de9b422aed0ce63fb7c75b17e91228249c6fdc7ee"} {"question" "q-27011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-27011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [5,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-3,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in [-5,-3]"] ["math" "x\\in [3,5]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-27011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [5,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+8x+8<x+2?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+8 & <x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+6 & <0 \\\\ (x+6)(x+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x+1)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=-1,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,-1),$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,-1)$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x+1)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x+1)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+8x+8<x+2$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-6,-1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81cf9097a0d21d1b4f4cc54de9b422aed0ce63fb7c75b17e91228249c6fdc7ee"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-278541" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x & \\le x+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+3x-18) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-3)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,3).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-6$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\le x+6$ for all $x\\in [-6,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81cf9097a0d21d1b4f4cc54de9b422aed0ce63fb7c75b17e91228249c6fdc7ee"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [6,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,-6]\\cup [3,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in [-3,6]"] ["math" "x\\in [-6,3]"] ["math" "x\\in [3,6]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "q-116190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in [-6,3]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/state.json" "file_sha256" "555e557661934952678d0b5c20850ad3771ad4731b37ee8d4fcfbb55b2151635"} {"question" "e-5191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5191.json" "file_sha256" "25a3a0119a6e08d031def303b472aa55afb13c98add9e9c65dab936c13ba4a75"} {"question" "e-5191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1),$ we choose a test point $x_1=-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-2-2=-4<0, \\\\ x+1 & =-2+1=-1<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1).$\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2),$ we can choose a test point $x_2=0.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0-2=(-), \\\\ x+1 & =0+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(-)\\times (+)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty),$ we can choose a test point $x_3=3.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-2=(+), \\\\ x+1 & =3+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(+)\\times (+)=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5191.json" "file_sha256" "25a3a0119a6e08d031def303b472aa55afb13c98add9e9c65dab936c13ba4a75"} {"question" "e-5174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt  0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5174.json" "file_sha256" "48804ca87f933e46c2f3f9efc0607a719fdb3309db2db1b33e806ffce5da8797"} {"question" "e-5174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & <0 \\\\ x(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0),$ $(0,3),$ and $(3,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5174.json" "file_sha256" "48804ca87f933e46c2f3f9efc0607a719fdb3309db2db1b33e806ffce5da8797"} {"question" "e-5175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5175.json" "file_sha256" "13e8843514504cf25f360fa573af58c7a65c429baacf6bde871646628dac4501"} {"question" "e-5175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-1)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5175.json" "file_sha256" "13e8843514504cf25f360fa573af58c7a65c429baacf6bde871646628dac4501"} {"question" "e-5176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5176.json" "file_sha256" "bdfa929c22892b2a26cc1ccc393ac8de20770ca2029d45f4d359093dd6b1231f"} {"question" "e-5176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & \\le 6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x+3) = 0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3),$ $(-3,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0$ for all $x\\in[-3,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13969014/example-5176.json" "file_sha256" "bdfa929c22892b2a26cc1ccc393ac8de20770ca2029d45f4d359093dd6b1231f"}] "usage" {"e-5175" [] "e-5176" [] "e-5174" [] "e-5191" [] "q-27068" [] "q-27071" [] "q-277702" []} "basis" 781 "retractions" [[17592186055104 :question/problem "Choose the appropriate signs for the sign table shown below.\n\n|  | $(- ∞,-11)$ | $(-11, 0)$ | $(0, ∞)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x + 11$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |"] [17592186055100 :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $x^{2} - 3x - 10 > 0$?"] [17592186055102 :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $3x^{2} - 3x - 36 \\le 0$?"] [17592186054473 :question/problem "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186054473 :question/worked-solution "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1)$, we choose a test point $x_1=-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2 &= -2 - 2 =-4 < 0, \\\\\nx + 1 &= -2 + 1 =-1 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1)$.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2)$, we can choose a test point $x_2=0$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 0 - 2 = (-),; x + 1|= 0 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (-) \\cdot (+) = (-)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty)$, we can choose a test point $x_3=3$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 3 - 2 = (+),; x + 1|= 3 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (+) \\cdot (+) = (+)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |"] [17592186054470 :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt 0$."] [17592186054470 :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 3) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0$. The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3$, and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0)$, $(0,3)$, and $(3,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3)$."] [17592186054471 :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0$."] [17592186054471 :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x + 2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x - 1)(x - 2) & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2)=0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2$, and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1)$, $(1,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty)$. Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $[MATH: x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty).]$"] [17592186054472 :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6$."] [17592186054472 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + x & \\le 6 \\\\ x^{2} + x - 6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x - 2)(x + 3) & \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) = 0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3$, and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3)$, $(-3,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2)$. Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in[-3,2]$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186052302 17592186052303 17592186052304 17592186052305 17592186052306 17592186052314 17592186052315 17592186052316 17592186052317 17592186052318 17592186052321 17592186052322 17592186052324 17592186052325 17592186052327 17592186052328] "immutable_fields" [[17592186052301 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052301 :answer-field/id] [17592186052301 :answer-field/key] [17592186052301 :answer-field/type] [17592186052313 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052313 :answer-field/id] [17592186052313 :answer-field/key] [17592186052313 :answer-field/type] [17592186052320 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052320 :answer-field/id] [17592186052320 :answer-field/key] [17592186052320 :answer-field/type] [17592186052323 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052323 :answer-field/id] [17592186052323 :answer-field/key] [17592186052323 :answer-field/type] [17592186052326 :answer-field/correct] [17592186052326 :answer-field/id] [17592186052326 :answer-field/key] [17592186052326 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
